{
  "dom_id": "step-q143140",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. The derivative at the point $(2,8)$ equals $5.$ What differential equation describes this curve?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis* is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\frac{1}{|x|}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k}{|x|}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=5$ when at the point $(2,8).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5=\\frac{k}{|2|}\\,⟹\\,k=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{|x|}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
